package ReviewCenter {
    part def SigningService {
        doc /* Adds, changes, and removes the reviewer slots of a review (only for a member or system administrator who can see the project; refused for an actor that came through a personal access token), records a signature after the signer, who must be a member of the project, has entered the password again (a failed re-authentication is audited; an actor that came through a token can never sign; a user of an OpenID Connect provider instead needs a live single-use proof of a new sign-in at the provider, spent when the signature is saved, and a missing proof is refused and audited; a signer with a second factor must also give a fresh code of the authenticator app, which is never a recovery code), and computes the approval status of a review from the record and its signatures, marking a slot assignee who is no longer a project member or whose account is disabled (`SlotMember.Enabled`), and carrying the counts behind the blockers (open threads, unanswered checks, failed checks) as numbers next to the blocker sentences. Offers the enabled members of a project as slot candidates, and assigns slots only to members. A review that every slot has signed for the current record is approved and locked: adding, changing, and removing a slot and signing are refused with the ReviewLocked rule (the shared helper `ReviewLock` holds the one definition: `IsConcluded` is true when every slot is signed for the current record and no thread is open; adding, changing, and removing slots and signing are refused with `ReviewLocked` once the review is concluded; an open thread, chat or included, also blocks signing with `SigningNotAllowed`, a rule separate from the digest comparison), and only an administrator of the project or a system administrator, never an actor that came through a token, can reopen it with a reason of one to 250 characters (`ReopenReviewAsync`, an audited `review.reopened` entry, the reopening kept as a `ReviewReopen`, listed with the approval); the earlier signatures then stay as history, out of date, and every slot must sign again. While the project requires independent reviewers, the user who created the review can neither be put in a slot nor sign it. While the project requires that the person who reopened a review does not sign it again (on by default, for every project), that user cannot sign a slot of the review (`ReopenerCannotSign`), and a reopen is refused when a slot would be left without anyone who may sign (`ReopenWouldStrandSlot`; the pure helper `ReopenRules` computes the stranded slots). With the helper `SignatureImpact`, it also computes which current signatures a change of the checklist or of the report content of a thread (an included comment, a promotion, a demotion, a resolution note) would make out of date, and the callers refuse the change with the SignaturesWouldBecomeOutOfDate rule, naming each affected slot number and signer, unless the caller acknowledges the slots. Also defines the slot, signature, role, approval, reopen, and status types of the Core subsystem. */

        comment sourceRef /* Source: src/DemaConsulting.ReviewCenter.Core/SigningService.cs, ReviewLock.cs, SignatureImpact.cs, ReopenRules.cs, ReviewReopen.cs, ReviewerSlot.cs, ReviewSignature.cs, SlotRole.cs, and ReviewApproval.cs */
        comment testRef /* Test: test/DemaConsulting.ReviewCenter.Tests/Core/SigningServiceTests.cs, SigningServiceExternalTests.cs, SigningServiceTotpTests.cs, ReopenRulesTests.cs, SigningServiceOpenThreadTests.cs, and AuditEventTests.cs (with the helper CountingUserStore.cs), plus ChecklistServiceTests.cs, ThreadServiceTests.cs and SignatureTestExtensions.cs for the signature impact */
        comment designRef /* Design: docs/design/review-center/core/signing-service.md */
        comment verificationRef /* Verification: docs/verification/review-center/core/signing-service.md */
        comment reqRef /* Requirements: docs/reqstream/review-center/core/signing-service.yaml */
    }
}
